# Deterministic fuse check for the print-in-place toggle clamp.
# The whole mechanism legitimately prints as ONE watertight body (four moving
# parts, all captured), so printcheck cannot see a weld: if any of the 0.4 mm
# Z films or 0.25 mm XY films bridge during printing, the part still slices
# green and the clamp arrives frozen. fusecheck answers it on the sliced STL:
# remove the arch zone — the flexure that connects nothing to nothing — and
# the print must still split into the 4 moving bodies it is supposed to be.
#
# Coordinates are design coordinates (lowest point of the print is z=0, the
# plate's bottom, so the rested frame is the model frame). The zone covers
# the arch beam (x -22.5..62.5, y -31.6..-24.4, z 7..12), its anchor posts,
# and the cam nub (z up to 15); it necessarily clips the cam tail's rim and
# the stop posts that merge with the left anchor post — both stay connected
# to their own bodies through material outside the zone.
flexure -28,-35,6.9:68,-20,16.6

# The sliced part, minus the arch zone, must split into 4 separable bodies:
# base, lever, link, carrier.
assert  over-center-toggle-clamp.stl 4

# Mandatory negative control: `part="fused"` zeroes every print-in-place
# clearance (films and cam gap), reproducing the weld as one solid. With the
# SAME zone removed it must STAY 1 body — proving the check can fire.
control fused 1
